  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   This strategy employs a combined optimization technique leveraging harmonic analysis and sparse binary codes for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The core idea is to represent the optimal step function as a sparse superposition of oscillatory patterns that inherently minimize self-overlap. This differs fundamentally from the parent strategy's heuristic binary flipping by incorporating higher mathematical structure from harmonic analysis.\n\n   Key innovations:\n   - Uses Ball and Cassels exponential sums to form candidate solutions that are theoretically optimal for low correlation\n   - Incorporates compressed sensing principles to find the sparsest representation meeting integral constraints\n   - Balances three objectives: minimizing maximum correlation, maintaining binary nature, and preserving periodic boundary conditions\n\n   Bottlenecks addressed:\n   - Escapes binary flipping local optima by using continuous harmonic representations\n   - Ensures global exploration with structured exponential basis\n   - Achieves theoretical guarantees on correlation bounds \n\n   Important caveat: This requires fundamental reconstruction from basic mathematical principles. The solution space is continuous in representation but discretized via projection. Potential limitation: Calculation of exponential sums could exceed precision for very large n, but our parameters keep this manageable.\n\n2. Math Specification:\n   Variables:\n   - x \u2208 \u211d\u207a        (Ball exponential parameter)\n   - \u03b8 \u2208 \u211d\u1d39        (Phase angles)\n   - w \u2208 \u2119^{2}     (Binary weights)\n\n   Objective:\n     Minimize C5 = (2/n) * sup_{k} |\u27e8h, 1-h(k + \u03b4)\u27e9|_{L^2([0,2])}\n\n   Constraints:\n     \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\n   Representation:\n     h(x) = \u2211_{j=1}^{M} w_j * exp(2\u03c0i * \u03b8_j * x / L)\n\n   For validation:\n     ||h||_\u221e \u2264 1\n     \u2211_{i} h[i] = n/2 (after projection)\n\n   The Fourier transform F{h} = \u2211_{j} w_j * exp(2\u03c0i\u03b8_j) \n\n   Special case: When h is piecewise constant, auto-correlation calculations reduce to subset matching problems.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Phase 1: Candidate generation\n     - Construct initial candidate population using:\n         exp_samp = ball_cassels_sequence(seed=seed, n=n, x=x0)\n     - Where ball_cassels_sequence(x)=sample real parts of exp(x*Z) that keep |z|\u22641\n\n   Phase 2: Adaptive filtering\n     - Precompute candidate set C\n     - score(c) = compute_c5(c, n)\n     - Filter C = {c \u2208 C | score(c) > best_score}\n     - Downsample if |C| > 100\n\n   Phase 3: Sparse projection\n     - Form optimal weights for each candidate: w = pseudoinverse_constrained(A, b)\n     - Where A is correlation matrix, b is integral internal constraint vector\n\n   Phase 4: Local optimization\n     - For each c in C:\n         h0 = binary_proj(c)\n         h = binary_local_search(h0, ...)\n         update_best(score(h))\n\n   Phase 5: Final quantization\n     - project_feasible(h, n) ensuring exact n/2 integral\n     - compute final score with redundant layers for validation\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Primary parameters: x \u2208 [1,5], \u03b8 \u2208 Gaussian(0, \u03c0\u00b2)\n   - Secondary parameters: w \u2208 simplex(0.4, 0.9)\n   - Pareto filtering: Keep top 3 candidates per phase\n   - Mont Carlo: 100 configurations per main phase\n\n5. Compute Budget:\n   - Population size: 20*(budget_s/200 s) rounds\n   - Valuation depth: 3 levels (score candidate once, scale by phase)\n   - Time allocation: 40% forward pass, 40% backtrack, 20% field updates\n\n6. Return contract:\n   - Interface: return best_h, best_c5, n\n   - Checks:\n        np.all(0<=best_h<=1)\n        abs(best_c5 - (2/n)*np.var(np.correlate(best_h, 1-best_h,'full')) < 1e-5\n        np.isclose(sum(best_h), n/2)",
